                    Atalla 0x01 — Cycle-Accurate Simulator

A cycle-accurate model of a heterogeneous SoC accelerator, built by Purdue
SoCET. It models the whole tensor datapath — DRAM through scratchpad, vector
core, and systolic array — with per-cycle timing for every component, so that
GEMM and tensor workloads can be measured (cycles, FLOP rate, bandwidth, queue
depths) before there is silicon to measure.

    Project      https://github.com/Purdue-SoCET/atalla/
    This repo    https://github.com/Purdue-SoCET/Atalla-Sim/
    Contact      socet@purdue.edu


THE MODELED SYSTEM
------------------

                  ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
                  │                    DRAM                       │
                  └───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘
                                          │ bursts
                  ┌───────────────────────▼───────────────────────┐
                  │                  Backend                      │
                  │        transaction queue, burst channel       │
                  └───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘
                                          │
                  ┌───────────────────────▼───────────────────────┐
                  │           Scratchpad  —  2 MB local           │
                  │   frontends ──► rd/wr queues ──► SRAM banks   │
                  │   (1 req/cyc)   (32 deep)     (4 pads x 0.5MB)│
                  │   pad 0 act │ pad 1 wgt │ pad 2 psum │ 3 out  │
                  │   one pad, one frontend, one backend, one VLSU│
                  └───────┬───────────────────────────▲───────────┘
                     load │                           │ store
                  ┌───────▼───────────────────────────┴───────────┐
                  │                 Vector Core                   │
                  │   scheduler issues one VLIW packet:           │
                  │     vlsu x4   load / store, one pad each      │
                  │     datapath  SIMD lanes + tree reduction     │
                  │     transpose tile transpose, rows <-> cols   │
                  │     gsau      feeds the systolic array        │
                  │   results return to Veggie (VRF) via wb_buffer│
                  └───────────────────────┬───────────────────────┘
                                          │ rows, via GSAU
                  ┌───────────────────────▼───────────────────────┐
                  │              SystolicArrayTPU                 │
                  │   grouped MAC cells, weight + psum shift      │
                  └───────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Every box is a `Clocked` object advanced once per simulated cycle. Boxes are
connected only by fixed-capacity `SimQueue` FIFOs, so backpressure propagates
by itself instead of being modeled explicitly.


REPOSITORY LAYOUT
-----------------

    src/
      base/          simulation framework: EventQueue, ClockDomain, Clocked,
                     wake groups, CompositeClocked, SimQueue, dtypes, debug
      scheduler/     the VLIW scheduler: fetch, icache, decode, execute,
                     data cache, vector dispatch; the ISA and the assembler
      memory/        DRAM, Backend, Scratchpad, Crossbar, Frontend, bus ports
      vector_core/   VectorCore, VLSU, VectorDatapath, GSAU, Veggie, transpose
      systolic_array/  MEISSA (the default) and the TPU-style array
      native/        optional C++ SIMD kernels (AVX2 / AVX-512)
      atalla/        platforms (scheduler-driven and harness-driven), the
                     performance monitor, experiment harnesses
    tests/           pytest unit and integration tests; tests/equivalence
                     holds the behaviour snapshot
    tools/           run_kernel.py, sweep runners, plotting, benchmarks
    third_party/     the C compiler and the functional simulator (submodules)
    docs/            see docs/README.md for the map
    logs/            simulation output (stats, gantt, schedule)
    Makefile  LICENSE  README


GETTING STARTED
---------------

    git clone --recurse-submodules git@github.com:Purdue-SoCET/atalla-sim.git
    pip install numpy pytest       # and matplotlib for the plotting tools
    make native        # optional but recommended; see below
    pytest tests/

The submodules (the compiler and the functional simulator) are private
Purdue-SoCET repositories. Without them the simulator still runs assembled
programs and the harnesses, and the tests that need them are skipped.

Run a program on the whole machine -- the scheduler, vector core, systolic
array, scratchpads and DRAM -- and get a cycle-by-cycle report:

    python tools/run_kernel.py relu.c --golden   # a C program, via the compiler
    python tools/run_kernel.py gemm              # an assembly kernel
    python tools/run_kernel.py prog.in           # an assembled program
    python tools/run_kernel.py gemm --activity gemm.html   # which units work when

docs/getting-started.md walks through installing, running, reading the
report and exploring a design.

Longer runs:

    # the 1024x1024 tiled GEMM: about an hour, left out of plain `pytest`
    pytest -m slow

    # parameter sweeps and plots
    python tools/run_sysarr_tpu_sweeps.py
    python tools/plot_sysarr_tpu_sweeps.py
    python tools/bench_native.py

Simulation output lands in `logs/`: stats.log, schedule.log, gemm_cycles.log,
gantt.log.


DOCUMENTATION
-------------

Start at `docs/README.md`. To run programs, read
`docs/architecture/scheduler.md`; to change a component,
`docs/architecture/base-classes.md`, which covers the tick/cycle model and
how to write a component.

    docs/architecture/   the scheduler, vector core, arrays, memory, and the
                         simulation framework they are built on
    docs/harnesses/      how experiments drive the platform
    docs/results/        measurements from specific runs
    docs/sweeps/         parameter studies
    docs/notebooks/      interactive scheduling model


HOW A CYCLE RUNS
----------------

    EventQueue ──► ClockDomain ──► platform.tick(t)
      (min-heap)     (one event         │
                      per cycle)        ├─ phase 10  VectorCore
                                        ├─ phase 20  VLS bridges
                                        ├─ phase 30  GSAU bridge ──► systolic array
                                        ├─ phase 40  Scratchpad
                                        │              pads: request port ─► banks
                                        ├─ phase 50  Backend / DRAM
                                        └─ phase 90  experiment harness

The event queue is a metronome: exactly one event is pending, and the clock
domain reschedules itself each cycle. Phase order is declared once, in
`src/atalla/sysarr_tpu_system.py`, rather than re-listed at every call site.


TICK SCHEDULING
---------------

Components are ticked only when they have work: a VLSU with empty queues, a
DMA backend with no transfer or an idle lane datapath sleeps until something
hands it work. Results are unchanged; runs are 18-37% faster on the kernels
and GEMM co-simulations (measured October 2026).

A clocked object says when it next needs to run, and is woken when work
reaches it from outside its own tick:

    def next_wake(self, now) -> Optional[int]      # None == asleep
    self.wake()                                    # called by whoever hands it work

The default returns `now + 1` ("tick me every cycle"), so a component that
does nothing special behaves exactly as before. Return None only when a tick
would change nothing, not even a counter; waking too late drops work silently.
A parent puts its children in a group with `add_child` and ticks them with
`tick_children` (the vector core does this for its units).

Scheduling must be invisible. Run the suite both ways and compare:

    ATALLA_SCHED=legacy pytest tests/     # tick everything, every cycle
    pytest tests/                         # default: skip idle components

`tests/equivalence` runs a kernel, a harness experiment, the transpose bench
and a tiled GEMM both ways against the same snapshot. The rules for making a
component sleep are in docs/architecture/base-classes.md, section 5.


BUILDING THE SIMD KERNELS
-------------------------

    make native

Compiles `src/native/atalla_kernels.cpp` into a shared object that accelerates
the systolic-array datapath and the fp16 and bf16 casts. Optional: without it the
simulator runs a pure-numpy fallback producing bit-identical results, just more
slowly. One .so carries AVX-512F, AVX2 and portable scalar paths and selects
one at load time via CPUID, so it can be built once and copied between hosts.

Measured on a 32x32 fp16 array (Xeon E5-2697A v4, AVX2, no AVX-512):

    pure Python (previous)   2070 us / simulated cycle
    numpy fallback            400 us / simulated cycle
    native AVX2                80 us / simulated cycle     ~25x

Reproduce the last two with `python tools/bench_native.py`.

To see which path is active, or to force the fallback:

    python3 -c "import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'src'); \
        from native import kernels; print(kernels.NATIVE_ISA)"
    ATALLA_NO_NATIVE=1 pytest tests/      # run without the .so

The kernels are held to bit-exact equivalence with the pure-Python reference:
no FMA (products are rounded before they are summed), double precision
throughout, and lane reductions kept in their original left-to-right order.
`tests/native/` checks the fp16 cast against numpy across every half-precision
midpoint and verifies the native and fallback datapaths tick identically.
`tests/base/test_dtype_bf16.py` checks every bf16 cast path against the
functional sim's `bf16_round` (numpy has no bfloat16).


NOTE
----

This project draws conceptual and structural inspiration from the
[gem5 Simulator](https://www.gem5.org/) project. Many architectural ideas —
the modular class hierarchy, naming conventions, and simulation lifecycle —
are based on the gem5 design.

